  --- p59 acceptance 3: nDCG@10, fused vs each half alone ---

    [A] EXACT-DUPLICATE LABELS (sha256) — INFORMATIONAL, CANNOT ADJUDICATE
      100 queries over exact-duplicate groups, 588 relevant documents in total
      (5.9 per query).

    arm                         nDCG@10
    text-only                    0.0000
    vector-only (exact)          1.0000
    FUSED                        0.9884
    delta vs text-only:   +0.9884
    delta vs vector-only: -0.0116

      -> THIS SET CANNOT ADJUDICATE p59 ACCEPTANCE 3, whatever it prints.
         Byte-identical files decode to identical pixels, so a real encoder
         gives them identical embeddings and the vector arm returns the whole
         group first: nDCG@10 = 1.0 BY CONSTRUCTION. `fused > vector` is then
         unreachable for a reason that has nothing to do with whether fusion
         works — nothing exceeds a perfect oracle. Reporting a FAIL from this
         set would be a verdict produced by the instrument, so it gets a
         [note] and set [B] below carries the verdict.

    [B] NEAR-DUPLICATE LABELS (dHash <= 4, sha256 DIFFERS) — THIS SET VOTES
      100 queries, one per distinct digest, 366 relevant documents in total
      (3.7 per query). The query document is fed its own indexed path text and
      its own embedding, and is excluded from its own results before scoring.
      NON-DEGENERATE BY CONSTRUCTION: the pixels differ, so the vector arm is
      strong but not perfect; the paths differ, so the text arm is independent;
      and the label comes from a perceptual hash, which is neither arm's own
      function, so it hands neither of them a free win.

    arm                         nDCG@10
    text-only                    0.0203
    vector-only (exact)          0.2910
    FUSED                        0.2932
    delta vs text-only:   +0.2729
    delta vs vector-only: +0.0021

      -> fusion strictly exceeds both halves on the NON-DEGENERATE set.
         p59 acceptance 3 HOLDS.

      LIMIT, stated beside the number rather than in a footnote: this measures
      NEAR-DUPLICATE RETRIEVAL, not semantic relevance. Nothing here shows that a
      fused query finds a semantically SIMILAR image, only that it finds the same
      image at another size or encoding. It is the strongest non-degenerate set
      this corpus can yield without human judgements — a scrape carries no
      relevance labels — and a real semantic evaluation needs a corpus with them.

