=== WHAT THIS RUN SURFACED =============================================
  1. AN IMAGE DOCUMENT WAS COLUMN-HUNGRIER THAN THE TEXT ENGINE'S BUDGET — FIXED.
     This corpus yields seven columns per image — path, format, orientation, colour
     bucket, width, height, byte length — and `index_text::MAX_FIELD` is 4. While a
     facet or a numeric column had to BE a scored field, three of the seven could not
     be indexed at all: orientation survived only as path text, and width and height
     had no numeric column, so neither could carry a hard range predicate. That was
     never a bench limitation to route around — the fused-query claim is one query
     plan over text, facet, numeric, vector and hash predicates, and it was bounded
     by how many predicates fit.
     This benchmark is what surfaced that collision, and the collision produced p65:
     UNSCORED COLUMNS. `Schema::with_column` declares a value that can be faceted,
     ranged or keyed on without spending a scored-field slot, and it costs zero bytes
     per posting because it is never tokenized.
     The fix worth naming is the one NOT taken. Widening `[u16; MAX_FIELD]` would put
     two more u16 on every posting of every existing text consumer to serve one image
     corpus. The engine instead stopped spending a SCORING slot on a value that
     nothing scores. MAX_FIELD is still 4; this run declares all seven columns,
     facets three of them and ranges the other three, width and height included.

  2. ON THIS CORPUS THE FILE EXTENSION DOES NOT LIE.
     `meta::sniff` disagreed with the extension on 0 of 17311 files (0.00%).
     That CONTRADICTS the expectation that scraped assets carry lying extensions.
     One Supabase-backed scrape is not the web: this host serves what it stored,
     and the scraper wrote the extension from the URL, so the two agree by
     construction. The sniff is still the right default — this run just does not
     produce evidence for it, and says so instead of quietly implying otherwise.

  3. THE NEAR-DUPLICATE RATE IS A CHOICE, NOT A MEASUREMENT.
     The dHash table above spans thresholds 0..12 and the PDQ table 0..48. Both move
     the answer by tens of percentage points. Any single headline rate for this
     corpus would be the threshold talking, which is exactly §5's warning.

